# MetaCollab API — Best Practices Analysis

> Scope: `app/api/**`, `services/**`, [lib/validations.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/validations.ts), [lib/utils.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts), `lib/models/**`

---

## 1. Architecture Overview

```
Client
  └── services/*.ts        (typed fetch wrappers — client side)
        └── lib/fetcher.ts (single HTTP client with timeout + error handling)
              └── app/api/**/route.ts   (Next.js App Router handlers)
                    ├── lib/validations.ts  (Zod schemas)
                    ├── lib/models/*.ts     (Mongoose models + interfaces)
                    └── lib/utils.ts        (shared helpers)
```

The layering is **correct and intentional**. The fetcher abstracts HTTP, services abstract endpoints, and routes handle auth + DB. This is a solid foundation.

---

## 2. Validation — What's Good, What's Missing

### ✅ What's working well

| Location | Validated with |
|---|---|
| `POST /api/projects` | `projectSchema` (Zod) |
| `PATCH /api/projects/[id]` | `projectUpdateSchema` with `.refine()` guard |
| `POST /api/tasks` | `taskSchema` (Zod) |
| `PATCH /api/tasks/[id]` | `taskUpdateSchema` (Zod, runs **before** DB now) |
| `POST /api/invitations` | `invitationSchema` with `.email()` format check |
| `PUT /api/users/[id]` | `userUpdateSchema` with `.refine()` guard |
| Invitation email | `.toLowerCase().trim()` normalised before storage |
| ObjectId inputs | [isValidObjectId()](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts#9-12) guard before every DB call |

### ❌ Missing Validation

#### `taskSchema` — `project` field is `z.string().min(1)` but never ObjectId-validated

```ts
// validations.ts — current
project: z.string().min(1, "Project ID is required"),

// better — validate it's a real ObjectId format at the schema level
project: z.string().refine(isMongoId, { message: "Invalid project ID" }),
```

The route does check membership via `Project.exists({ _id: projectId })` which would throw a CastError on a bad ID, but Zod should be the first line of defence.

#### `taskUpdateSchema` and `projectUpdateSchema` — no `.refine()` check that at least one field was sent for update

`projectUpdateSchema` already has this `.refine()`. `taskUpdateSchema` doesn't:

```ts
// current — PATCH /api/tasks/[id] will accept an empty {} body silently
export const taskUpdateSchema = taskSchema.partial().omit({ project: true }).extend({...});

// better
export const taskUpdateSchema = taskSchema.partial().omit({ project: true }).extend({
  order: z.number().optional(),
  assigneeId: z.string().nullable().optional(),
  dueDate: z.string().nullable().optional(),
}).refine(
  (data) => Object.values(data).some((v) => v !== undefined),
  { message: "At least one field must be provided" }
);
```

#### `GET /api/users` — no email format validation before Clerk lookup

```ts
// current — raw query string passed directly to Clerk
const email = req.nextUrl.searchParams.get("email");
// ...
await client.users.getUserList({ emailAddress: [email] });

// better — validate format first
const emailParsed = z.string().email().safeParse(email);
if (!emailParsed.success) {
  return NextResponse.json({ message: "Invalid email format" }, { status: 400 });
}
```

#### `POST /api/projects/[id]/chat` — message length check has a logic bug

```ts
// CURRENT — the length check is INSIDE the "message is required" block
// message.length will throw if message is undefined or not a string
if (!message || typeof message !== "string" || !message.trim()) {
    if (message.length > 2000) {  // ← BUG: never reached when message is empty/non-string
        ...
    }
    return NextResponse.json({ message: "Message is required" }, { status: 400 });
}

// CORRECT order — length check AFTER the type guard
if (!message || typeof message !== "string" || !message.trim()) {
    return NextResponse.json({ message: "Message is required" }, { status: 400 });
}
if (message.length > 2000) {
    return NextResponse.json({ message: "Message cannot exceed 2000 characters" }, { status: 400 });
}
```

> This is an actual **runtime bug** — the 2000-char guard never runs.

---

## 3. Code Duplication

### Already fixed ✅
- [isValidObjectId](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts#9-12) extracted to [lib/utils.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts) and imported in 5 files
- [resolveRole()](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/app/api/users/%5Bid%5D/route.ts#7-11) extracted inside `users/[id]/route.ts`

### Still duplicated ❌

#### Auth boilerplate — repeated in every single route handler (×11)

```ts
const { userId } = await auth();
if (!userId) {
  return NextResponse.json({ message: "Unauthorized" }, { status: 401 });
}
```

**Recommendation:** Extract a `requireAuth()` helper to `lib/api.ts`:

```ts
// lib/api.ts
import { auth } from "@clerk/nextjs/server";
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";

export async function requireAuth(): Promise<
  { userId: string; error: null } | { userId: null; error: NextResponse }
> {
  const { userId } = await auth();
  if (!userId) {
    return {
      userId: null,
      error: NextResponse.json({ message: "Unauthorized" }, { status: 401 }),
    };
  }
  return { userId, error: null };
}
```

Usage becomes:
```ts
const { userId, error } = await requireAuth();
if (error) return error;
```

#### Validation error response — repeated in 6 files

```ts
return NextResponse.json(
  { message: parsed.error.errors[0]?.message ?? "Invalid input" },
  { status: 400 }
);
```

**Recommendation:** Add a helper:

```ts
// lib/api.ts
export function validationError(error: z.ZodError): NextResponse {
  return NextResponse.json(
    { message: error.errors[0]?.message ?? "Invalid input" },
    { status: 400 }
  );
}
```

#### Generic 500 error handler — repeated in 11 files

```ts
catch (error: unknown) {
  console.error("[ROUTE] error:", error);
  return NextResponse.json({ message: "Internal server error" }, { status: 500 });
}
```

**Recommendation:** Add a helper:

```ts
// lib/api.ts
export function serverError(route: string, error: unknown): NextResponse {
  console.error(`[${route}] error:`, error);
  return NextResponse.json({ message: "Internal server error" }, { status: 500 });
}
```

#### [resolveRole](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/app/api/users/%5Bid%5D/route.ts#7-11) — defined inside `users/[id]/route.ts` but admin route also reads `publicMetadata.role`

Move [resolveRole](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/app/api/users/%5Bid%5D/route.ts#7-11) to [lib/utils.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts) since [admin/route.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/app/api/admin/route.ts) compares `userRole !== "admin"` using the same raw untyped value.

#### Status/priority enum literals — duplicated in Zod schemas AND Mongoose schemas AND service interfaces

```ts
// In Zod (validations.ts)
status: z.enum(["todo", "in-progress", "done"])

// In Mongoose (Task.ts)
enum: ["todo", "in-progress", "done"]

// In service types (taskService.ts)
status: "todo" | "in-progress" | "done"
```

**Recommendation:** Define shared `const` enums in a central types file:

```ts
// lib/types.ts
export const TASK_STATUSES = ["todo", "in-progress", "done"] as const;
export type TaskStatus = typeof TASK_STATUSES[number];

export const TASK_PRIORITIES = ["low", "medium", "high"] as const;
export type TaskPriority = typeof TASK_PRIORITIES[number];
```

Then consume everywhere:
```ts
// Zod
status: z.enum(TASK_STATUSES)

// Service interface
status: TaskStatus

// Mongoose enum
enum: [...TASK_STATUSES]
```

---

## 4. Type Mismatches Between Layers

| Service type | API/Model reality | Impact |
|---|---|---|
| `Task.id: string` | Mongoose returns `_id` (ObjectId) unless `toJSON` transform runs — `.lean()` skips the transform | Service may receive `_id` instead of [id](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts#9-12) |
| `Project.id: string` | Same issue — `GET /api/projects` uses `.lean()` without the transform | Frontend could receive `_id` not [id](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts#9-12) |
| `Invitation.id: string` | `GET /api/invitations` uses `.lean()` — transform won't run | Same `_id` / [id](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts#9-12) mismatch |
| `ITask.project: string` | Should be `mongoose.Types.ObjectId` or `Schema.Types.ObjectId` for proper ref typing | Stored as string, no referential integrity |
| `ITask.dueDate?: Date` | Service types it as `string`, Zod validates it as `string`, but model stores it as `Date` | Timezone and parsing inconsistencies |

> **Fix for `.lean()` mismatch:** Either stop using `.lean()` and call `.toJSON()`, or add a helper that explicitly serialises `_id → id`:
```ts
// lib/api.ts
export function toJSON<T extends { _id?: unknown }>(doc: T) {
  const { _id, __v, ...rest } = doc as Record<string, unknown>;
  return { id: _id, ...rest };
}
```

---

## 5. Service Layer Best Practices

### ✅ What's good
- Single [fetcher.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/fetcher.ts) with timeout, error extraction, and `.data` unwrapping — clean and DRY
- All services typed with `interface` for inputs and outputs
- `encodeURIComponent` used correctly in query params ([getTasks](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/taskService.ts#39-46), [getUserByEmail](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/userService.ts#18-25))

### ❌ Issues

#### [fetcher.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/fetcher.ts) — `Content-Type: application/json` always set, even for GET requests (no body)

Sending a `Content-Type` header on a GET is harmless but technically incorrect. Some servers and proxies reject it.

```ts
// better — only set Content-Type when there's a body
const hasBody = options.body != null;
headers: {
  ...(hasBody && { "Content-Type": "application/json" }),
  ...(options.headers as Record<string, string>),
},
```

#### [fetcher.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/fetcher.ts) — `AbortController` timeout (10s) is hardcoded

```ts
// current
const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10_000);

// better — accept as an option with a sensible default
const timeout = options.signal ? undefined : 10_000;
```

Also, if the caller passes their own `signal`, the current code overwrites it.

#### [reorderTasks](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/taskService.ts#74-84) in [taskService.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/taskService.ts) — fires N individual PATCH requests in parallel

```ts
// current — N round-trips for N tasks after drag-and-drop
await Promise.all(reorderedTasks.map((task) => updateTask(task.id, { order: task.order })));
```

This is a common anti-pattern for bulk operations. A proper `/api/tasks/reorder` endpoint should accept the full `[{ id, order }]` array and do a single `bulkWrite` in MongoDB.

#### [invitationService.ts](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/invitationService.ts) — [inviteMember](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/invitationService.ts#39-49) returns `Promise<void>` but the API returns the created invitation

```ts
// current
export const inviteMember = async (...): Promise<void>

// better — return the created invitation so the caller can react/display it
export const inviteMember = async (...): Promise<Invitation>
```

---

## 6. Model Best Practices

### ✅ What's good
- `timestamps: true` on all models
- Compound indexes on frequently queried combinations (`project + status`, `project + recipient + status`)
- TTL index on `Invitation.expiresAt` — invitations auto-expire, nice
- Partial unique index on [Invitation](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/invitationService.ts#4-13) for pending duplicates
- `toJSON` transforms clean `_id`, `__v`, and `token` from output
- `sparse: true` on `Task.assignee` — avoids null-value unique conflicts

### ❌ Issues

#### [User](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/userService.ts#4-11) model exists but is never used by any API route

All user data is fetched directly from Clerk. The [User](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/services/userService.ts#4-11) model (with `clerkUserId`, `name`, `email`) is defined but no route ever reads from or writes to it. This is dead code — either:
- Delete it and rely solely on Clerk, OR
- Keep it as a local cache and sync it via a Clerk webhook

#### `ITask.project` typed as `string` instead of [ObjectId](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/utils.ts#9-12)

```ts
// current — no referential integrity
project: string;

// better
project: mongoose.Types.ObjectId;
```

Same for `IInvitation.project`, `IInvitation.inviter`.

#### [ITask](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/models/Task.ts#3-13) / [IProject](file:///e:/Yash%20Interview%20Projectz/MetaCollab/lib/models/Project.ts#8-16) don't include `createdAt` / `updatedAt` in the interface

Even though `timestamps: true` adds these fields, they're not in the TypeScript interface. TypeScript won't know they exist.

```ts
export interface ITask extends Document {
  // ...
  createdAt: Date;  // add these
  updatedAt: Date;
}
```

---

